<title>COTE D'IVOIRE: Ivory Coast lowers rice import tariffs.</title>
<headline>Ivory Coast lowers rice import tariffs.</headline>
<dateline>ABIDJAN 1997-08-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>Ivory Coast has cut cargo rice import charges to a total of five percent from 15 percent to raise capacity utilisation and employment at Ivorian rice factories, a government said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>Paddy rice had been zero-rated from previous tax and customs rates totalling ten percent, the statement added in the government daily Fraternite Matin.</p>
<p>Cargo rice taxes before the change, set in August last year, were 10 percent, with an additional customs rate of five percent, it said.</p>
<p>Ivory Coast has said it aims to be self-sufficient in rice, but imports still account for around 50 percent of consumption.</p>
<p>Market researchers say lack of organisation by Ivorian farmers contrasts with well-targeted marketing campaigns by importers offering a wider range of products.</p>
<p>-- Matthew Bunce, Abidjan newsroom + 225 21 90 90</p>
</text>
